<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/movement-and-the-motor-system">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Movement looks simple and is the brain's hardest computation: <strong>converting intention
    into the exact contraction of hundreds of muscles with millisecond timing</strong>. Signing
    your name recruits dozens of muscles in each arm in a pattern that never repeats exactly
    twice, yet your signature is recognisable every time. The nervous system manages this with a
    hierarchy of specialised controllers — cortex for strategy and command, basal ganglia for
    selection and initiation, cerebellum for calibration, spinal cord and muscles for
    execution — and each level's failure produces a different, diagnosable signature. The other
    deep surprise is that the motor system never issues a command and hopes: it predicts, acts,
    senses the result, and corrects continuously, in loops running at several different speeds.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the motor hierarchy and what each level contributes" widget="equationStep" config={motorCfg} />

  <h2>Motor units and muscle receptors: the final common path</h2>
  <p>
    Sherrington called the alpha motor neuron the <strong>final common path</strong>: whatever
    the cortex, reflexes or spinal circuits decide, the only way to act on a muscle is through
    one of these neurons. One motor neuron plus all the muscle fibres it innervates forms a
    <strong>motor unit</strong> — the indivisible quantum of force, since all its fibres
    contract together. The size of the unit sets the resolution of control. Extraocular muscles
    that aim the eyes have motor units of roughly a dozen fibres each, giving the fine gradation
    eye movements need; the gastrocnemius (calf) has units of 1,000-2,000 fibres, favouring
    power over precision. Control is a numbers game in two dimensions: <em>recruitment</em>
    (which units fire) and <em>rate coding</em> (how fast each fires). Henneman's
    <strong>size principle</strong> organises recruitment: small, slow, fatigue-resistant units
    are always hired first, large fast-fatigable units only when demand rises — which is why
    standing still is metabolically cheap and a sprint is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Muscles are also <strong>sensor organs</strong>, and the two embedded sensors answer
    different questions. The <strong>muscle spindle</strong>, lying in parallel with the main
    fibres, reports muscle <em>length</em> and, above all, the <em>velocity</em> of stretch via
    its fast Ia afferents. The <strong>Golgi tendon organ</strong>, sitting in series at the
    junction of muscle and tendon, reports <em>tension</em> (force). One measures position and
    speed, the other load. A small side-loop, the <strong>gamma system</strong>, keeps the
    spindle taut during contraction so it stays sensitive at every muscle length — without it,
    shortening a muscle would silence its own sensor.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> muscles are dumb actuators that merely receive commands.
  In fact about a third of the nervous system's traffic with muscle is <em>upward</em>: spindle
  and tendon afferents give the spinal cord and cortex a continuous report of limb position,
  speed and load. Deprived of it (large-fibre sensory neuropathy does exactly this), people
  cannot coordinate a reach without watching their hands — the famous patient I.W. had to
  relearn movement visually, controlling each joint by eye.</p>

  <h2>Spinal reflexes: the fast lane that bypasses the brain</h2>
  <p>
    The patellar reflex is the cleanest worked example in neuroscience. A tap on the tendon
    stretches the quadriceps; spindles fire; Ia afferents race to the spinal cord at up to
    80-120 metres per second; a <em>single</em> synapse excites the alpha motor neurons of the
    same muscle; the quadriceps contracts. Total loop time: about 50 milliseconds. Conscious
    perception of the tap alone would take around 200 milliseconds of cortical processing — a
    reflex that consulted the cortex would arrive long after the knee buckled. Evolution's
    solution: hard-wire the most urgent corrections into the cord. The same logic scales up in
    the <strong>withdrawal reflex</strong>: step on a tack and pain afferents trigger a
    polysynaptic circuit that flexes the hurt leg <em>and</em>, via commissural interneurons,
    extends the opposite leg to keep you upright (<strong>crossed extensor reflex</strong>) —
    two-legged balance demands the pair.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two subtleties matter. First, reflexes are <strong>modulated, not fixed</strong>: descending
    pathways continuously re-scale their gain, which is why the same tap feels different when
    you are relaxed, braced or startled, and why clinicians use reflex testing as a probe —
    hyperreflexia above a spinal lesion, absent reflexes at it. Second, reflex circuits do
    housekeeping for voluntary movement: <strong>reciprocal inhibition</strong> (Ia
    interneurons suppressing the antagonist when the agonist contracts) runs during every
    voluntary action, offloading muscular bookkeeping from the cortex.
  </p>

  <h2>Descending pathways: from cortex to cord</h2>
  <p>
    Voluntary commands travel the <strong>corticospinal (pyramidal) tract</strong>: cell bodies
    in layer V of motor cortex send roughly a million axons through the internal capsule, down
    the brainstem, and across at the <strong>medullary pyramids</strong> — about 85% of them
    decussate to form the lateral tract controlling distal limb muscles. The crossing explains
    two bedside facts: a left-hemisphere stroke paralyses the right side, and the <em>level</em>
    of a spinal cord injury predicts the loss (damage at C4 takes all four limbs; at T6 it
    spares the arms). Sister tracts fill complementary roles — reticulospinal for posture and
    muscle tone, vestibulospinal for balance, tectospinal for turning head and eyes, all
    descending from the brainstem rather than cortex.
  </p>
  <p>
    The lesion vocabulary you need: <strong>upper motor neuron</strong> damage (cortex,
    internal capsule, tract) produces weakness with <em>increased</em> tone, brisk reflexes and
    the upgoing Babinski sign — the brake is gone. <strong>Lower motor neuron</strong> damage
    (the alpha neuron or its axon) produces flaccid weakness, wasting, fasciculations and absent
    reflexes — the engine is gone. Every motor disease sorts into this grid, which is what makes
    the neurology exam so powerful.
  </p>

  <h2>Motor cortex: maps, votes and the readiness potential</h2>
  <p>
    Penfield's electrical mapping exposed the <strong>somatotopic map</strong>: neighbouring
    body parts are controlled by neighbouring cortex, with grotesque distortion — hands and
    lips command a hugely disproportionate share of tissue, in proportion to the precision they
    demand, not their size. But the map is not a switchboard. Georgopoulos' classic recordings
    showed a single M1 neuron fires for a <em>range</em> of reach directions (broadly,
    cosine-tuned around its favourite); the actual movement direction is read out from the
    <strong>population vector</strong> — thousands of neurons voting, the brain averaging away
    single-neuron noise. Neighbouring cortical areas divide the planning: the
    <strong>premotor cortex</strong> specialises in externally cued actions (reach when the
    light goes green), the <strong>supplementary motor area</strong> in internally generated
    sequences (play the piece from memory).
  </p>
  <p>
    The timing experiments are the strangest result in the field. Kornhuber and Deecke (1965)
    recorded a <strong>readiness potential</strong> building over SMA up to a second or more
    before a self-timed finger movement. Libet (1983) added the twist: subjects reported
    becoming <em>aware</em> of their decision only about 200 milliseconds before moving. The
    brain began preparing the action long before consciousness entered the room — which does
    not settle free will (Libet himself proposed a final veto), but it demolished the naive
    picture of a self issuing commands into a passive body.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the homunculus map means each muscle has one dedicated
  brain cell. M1 does not work in muscle-language at all — it encodes movements, directions and
  forces at the population level, which is precisely why brain-computer interfaces can decode
  an intended reach from a few hundred neurons and drive a robotic arm with it.</p>

  <h2>Basal ganglia: the gatekeeper</h2>
  <p>
    The basal ganglia — striatum (caudate and putamen), globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus
    and substantia nigra — form the loop that <strong>selects and releases</strong> actions.
    The core trick is disinhibition. In the <strong>direct pathway</strong>, cortex excites
    striatal neurons (D1 receptors) that inhibit the output nuclei (GPi/SNr), which normally
    hold the thalamus in check; free the thalamus, and movement is released. The
    <strong>indirect pathway</strong> (D2 receptors, via the external pallidum and subthalamic
    nucleus) does the reverse, amplifying the brake. Dopamine from the substantia nigra biases
    the balance towards "go", which is how the system implements selection, initiation and —
    through the same circuitry — habit learning: the chunked stimulus-response routines you can
    do while thinking of something else.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pathology proves the design. Kill the dopamine neurons (Parkinson's disease — symptoms
    appear only after roughly 60-80% are lost) and the brake wins: <em>hypokinesia</em>,
    slowness of initiation, rigidity and the classic 4-6 Hz resting tremor. Treatment follows
    the circuit: L-DOPA tops up the missing transmitter, and deep brain stimulation of the
    subthalamic nucleus or GPi re-tunes the loop electrically. Break the <em>indirect</em>
    pathway instead — as Huntington's disease does to the caudate (a CAG-repeat genetic
    degeneration, onset age tracking repeat length) — and the gate swings open:
    <em>hyperkinesia</em>, the writhing involuntary chorea. One circuit, two opposite diseases,
    depending on which wire fails.
  </p>

  <h2>Cerebellum: the error corrector and predictor</h2>
  <p>
    The cerebellum is about 10% of brain volume but holds the majority of the brain's neurons —
    tens of billions of tiny granule cells feeding the magnificent Purkinje cells, each with
    over 100,000 synapses and the sole output of cerebellar cortex (inhibitory, onto the deep
    nuclei). Its job is calibration. It receives a copy of the intended movement from cortex
    and a stream of sensory reports of what actually happened, and it issues corrections on a
    millisecond clock — faster than sensory feedback could travel the round trip. In
    computational terms it runs a <em>forward model</em>: it predicts the sensory consequence
    of the command and flags the mismatch. Learning follows the same circuit: climbing fibres
    carry the error signal, and long-term depression at the parallel-fibre-Purkinje synapse
    (the Marr-Albus-Ito model) slowly retunes the prediction — the mechanism behind adapting to
    new glasses, learning to catch, or a tennis serve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Damage produces a signature everyone has seen in miniature after alcohol (which
    transiently impairs the cerebellum): <strong>ataxia</strong> — jerky, staggered movement
    with <em>normal strength</em>; <strong>dysmetria</strong>, overshooting or undershooting a
    target; <strong>intention tremor</strong>, which worsens as the hand approaches the cup —
    the exact opposite of Parkinson's rest tremor; and slurred, scanning speech. No paralysis:
    the gate still opens, the commands still flow, but they arrive uncalibrated.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the cerebellum is "just" a movement organ. Its inputs and
  outputs connect to most of cortex, and imaging plus lesion studies now implicate it in
  language processing, timing, and predicting abstract sequences — a general-purpose
  prediction machine that happens to have been discovered through movement.</p>

  <h2>Reading the lesions: a worked diagnostic exercise</h2>
  <p>
    Put the signatures together and you can localise like a neurologist. <em>Case 1:</em>
    weakness of the right face and arm with brisk reflexes and an upgoing toe — corticospinal
    territory, left hemisphere: expect a stroke in the internal capsule, where a vessel a
    millimetre wide carries every fibre from the cortex. <em>Case 2:</em> slow initiation,
    rigidity, rest tremor — basal ganglia (Parkinson's). <em>Case 3:</em> jerky, inaccurate
    movement, intention tremor, normal strength — cerebellum. <em>Case 4:</em> wasting,
    fasciculations <em>and</em> spasticity in the same limbs, with sensation spared —
    <strong>amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</strong>, the rare disease that kills both upper and
    lower motor neurons while leaving the sensory system untouched. <em>Case 5:</em> loss of
    movement and position sense on one side of the body with pain-temperature loss on the
    other — a hemisection of the cord (Brown-Séquard syndrome), where each fibre type crosses
    at a different level. Five cases, five addresses, all from the same map.
  </p>

  <h2>The ocular motor system: the fastest movements you make</h2>
  <p>
    The retina samples the world sharply only at the <strong>fovea</strong>, a patch about 1-2
    degrees wide resolving roughly one arc-minute — so the eyes must be <em>aimed</em> at
    whatever matters and <em>stabilised</em> on it. Six extraocular muscles per eye do the
    work, wired by three cranial nerves: the oculomotor (III) drives most of them, the
    trochlear (IV) the superior oblique, the abducens (VI) the lateral rectus — which is why a
    sixth-nerve palsy leaves an eye that will not abduct, a classic exam finding.
  </p>
  <p>
    Five systems share the hardware, and each has a tell. <strong>Saccades</strong> snap the
    fovea to a new target: ballistic (no mid-course correction), reaching peak velocities up to
    about 900 degrees per second, after a latency of around 200 ms. During each saccade the
    brain <em>suppresses vision</em> (saccadic masking) — you are effectively blind for the
    20-80 ms of flight, and the system edits the gap out, which is why you can never see your
    own eyes move in a mirror. <strong>Smooth pursuit</strong> holds a moving target on the
    fovea, tracking accurately up to roughly 30-40 degrees per second — and it needs a moving
    target to exist; asked to move the eyes smoothly across a stationary scene, they will only
    produce a staircase of small saccades. <strong>The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR)</strong>
    is the stabiliser: a three-neuron arc from the inner ear that counter-rotates the eyes for
    every head motion, fast enough to keep the world steady while you jog, its gain fine-tuned
    by the cerebellum (the textbook case of cerebellar motor learning). <strong>Vergence</strong>
    converges or diverges the eyes for near and far; <strong>fixation</strong> holds the line —
    punctuated by microsaccades that keep neurons from adapting to a perfectly static image.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reading is an ocular-motor behaviour you are performing right now: a train of saccades
    about 7-9 characters long, punctuated by fixations of roughly 200-250 ms, with regressions
    back over hard passages. Eye-tracking experiments (McConkie & Rayner's moving-window
    studies) show the useful span of text is a narrow window around fixation — the mental
    machinery of reading is coupled, millisecond by millisecond, to the machinery that aims the
    eyes.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the eyes glide smoothly across a page or scan a room like a
  camera pan. Almost every large eye movement is a saccade — a jump — followed by a stop.
  True smooth motion exists only when tracking something that is itself moving, or when the
  VOR is countering your own head motion.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>From conscious to automatic.</strong> New skills run through cortex and demand
    attention; practice shifts control towards cerebellum, basal ganglia and spinal circuits —
    smooth, fast, effortless. "Muscle memory" is neural: the muscles learned nothing. Mirror
    neurons (discovered 1996 in macaque premotor cortex) fire both when acting and when
    watching the same action, and are candidates for the substrate of imitation and empathy —
    though how far the claim extends in humans is actively debated. The cortical body map is
    use-dependent: the hand area enlarges in string players; amputees feel phantom limbs in
    cortical real estate that neighbouring inputs have quietly annexed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Motor neuroscience meets engineering.</strong> Brain-computer interfaces (BrainGate,
    Neuralink) decode population activity in M1 to drive cursors and robotic arms — the
    population-vector insight made silicon. On the clinical frontier, the lesion grid keeps
    paying: deep brain stimulation of the basal ganglia loop, once heretical, is standard care
    in advanced Parkinson's; VOR-training protocols rehabilitate vestibular patients by
    deliberately driving cerebellar gain adaptation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Why does the cerebellum hold so many neurons for so
    modest a volume — is timing the missing variable in theories of cognition? Does basal
    ganglia dopamine encode reward, movement vigour, or both (the "motivation vs motor" debate
    is live)? And how much of what we call intelligence is, at bottom, sensorimotor prediction?
    The motor system, long the poor relation of sensory neuroscience, is where the field now
    expects the next surprises.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kandel et al., Principles of Neural Science; Sherrington's "final common path" (The Integrative Action of the Nervous System, 1906); Henneman's size principle; Georgopoulos et al. on population coding; Libet et al. (1983) on readiness potentials; Azevedo et al. (2009) on cell numbers; Shadmehr & Wise, The Computational Neurobiology of Reaching and Pointing; Rizzolatti on mirror neurons.</p>

